<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>Self-study quiz.</strong> This quiz is for your own review — answers are not recorded. You’ll get instant feedback on each question.</p>
</div></div>

<div class="quiz"><div class="quiz__top-bar"><div class="quiz__score-tracker" style="display:none;"></div><button class="quiz__start-over" type="button" style="display:none;">Start Over</button></div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q1b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">1</span> Who was the most direct intellectual influence on Mill's utilitarianism?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1a" id="q1a">
        <label for="q1a">Thomas Aquinas</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1b" id="q1b">
        <label for="q1b">Jeremy Bentham</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1c" id="q1c">
        <label for="q1c">David Hume</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q1" value="q1d" id="q1d">
        <label for="q1d">Immanuel Kant</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Jeremy Bentham, a close friend of Mill's father, founded classical utilitarianism. Mill was raised as Bentham's intellectual heir and spent much of his career revising Bentham's views.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Mill's utilitarianism grew directly out of the work of a family friend who raised him in it. Revisit Mill's Life and Context.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q2d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">2</span> In Chapter 1, Mill concedes that:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2a" id="q2a">
        <label for="q2a">Happiness is not actually the only intrinsic good</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2b" id="q2b">
        <label for="q2b">Utilitarianism has been definitively refuted by Kant</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2c" id="q2c">
        <label for="q2c">Utilitarian calculations are too difficult to perform reliably</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q2" value="q2d" id="q2d">
        <label for="q2d">The principle of utility cannot be proved in the strict logical sense, because ultimate principles cannot be derived from anything more basic</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Mill grants that first principles cannot be proved in the strict sense, because there is nothing more basic to derive them from. He offers considerations that can move a rational mind instead.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Mill does not abandon utilitarianism in Chapter 1. Ask what he says about whether any ultimate principle can be strictly proved.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q3c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">3</span> The Greatest Happiness Principle holds that actions are right insofar as they:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3a" id="q3a">
        <label for="q3a">Follow the natural law as accessible to human reason</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3b" id="q3b">
        <label for="q3b">Conform to a universal moral law</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3c" id="q3c">
        <label for="q3c">Tend to promote happiness, wrong as they tend to produce the reverse of happiness</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q3" value="q3d" id="q3d">
        <label for="q3d">Express a virtuous character</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. The Greatest Happiness Principle: actions are right in proportion as they tend to promote happiness, and wrong as they tend to produce the reverse of happiness.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Mill's principle judges actions by their consequences for happiness. The other options describe Kant, virtue ethics, and Aquinas.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q4a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">4</span> Mill's distinction between higher and lower pleasures is intended to:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4a" id="q4a">
        <label for="q4a">Refine Bentham's quantitative hedonism by recognizing that some pleasures are qualitatively more valuable than others</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4b" id="q4b">
        <label for="q4b">Justify a class system in which some pleasures are reserved for educated people</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4c" id="q4c">
        <label for="q4c">Show that happiness is not the only intrinsic good</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q4" value="q4d" id="q4d">
        <label for="q4d">Replace utilitarianism with a non-hedonistic ethics</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Bentham counted pleasures by quantity alone. Mill adds quality: some pleasures, such as those of the intellect and moral sentiments, are more valuable in kind, not just in amount.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Mill is refining Bentham, not abandoning hedonism. Ask what Mill adds to Bentham's purely quantitative measure of pleasure.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q5c">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">5</span> Mill's famous remark that "it is better to be a human being dissatisfied than a pig satisfied" is part of his response to which objection?</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5a" id="q5a">
        <label for="q5a">That utilitarianism cannot account for justice</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5b" id="q5b">
        <label for="q5b">That utilitarianism requires us to calculate consequences before every action</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5c" id="q5c">
        <label for="q5c">That utilitarianism is "a doctrine fit only for swine" because it makes pleasure the goal of morality</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q5" value="q5d" id="q5d">
        <label for="q5d">That utilitarianism cannot be proved</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Critics called utilitarianism a 'doctrine worthy only of swine.' Mill replies that human beings have higher faculties, and anyone who has experienced both kinds of pleasure prefers the higher, even with dissatisfaction.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Mill's remark answers a specific charge about pleasure, that a pleasure-based ethics degrades human beings. Ask which objection that is.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q6d">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">6</span> To say utilitarianism is aggregative means:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6a" id="q6a">
        <label for="q6a">It collects evidence inductively rather than deducing from first principles</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6b" id="q6b">
        <label for="q6b">It evaluates the moral worth of agents rather than actions</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6c" id="q6c">
        <label for="q6c">It applies only to groups, not to individuals</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q6" value="q6d" id="q6d">
        <label for="q6d">It sums pleasures and pains across all affected persons to evaluate an action</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Utilitarianism adds up happiness and unhappiness across everyone affected. An action is judged by its total effect, not by its effect on any one person.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">'Aggregative' means adding together. Ask what utilitarianism adds up when it evaluates an action.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q7b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">7</span> The key difference between act utilitarianism and rule utilitarianism is that:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7a" id="q7a">
        <label for="q7a">Act utilitarianism considers only short-term consequences while rule utilitarianism considers long-term consequences only</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7b" id="q7b">
        <label for="q7b">Act utilitarianism applies the principle of utility to individual actions, while rule utilitarianism applies it to general rules of conduct</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7c" id="q7c">
        <label for="q7c">Act utilitarianism applies to individuals while rule utilitarianism applies to societies</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q7" value="q7d" id="q7d">
        <label for="q7d">Act utilitarianism is consequentialist while rule utilitarianism is deontological</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Act utilitarianism asks which particular action produces the most happiness. Rule utilitarianism asks which general rules would produce the most happiness if followed, then judges actions by those rules.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Both versions are consequentialist. The difference is what the principle of utility is applied to: each action, or general rules of conduct.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q8b">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">8</span> To the objection that we cannot predict the consequences of our actions, Mill's response is:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8a" id="q8a">
        <label for="q8a">That we should perform every action and then evaluate after the fact</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8b" id="q8b">
        <label for="q8b">That we know enough about typical consequences to act sensibly, and we follow inherited moral rules precisely because past experience has shown they tend to maximize happiness</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8c" id="q8c">
        <label for="q8c">That utilitarianism is only meant to apply in cases of clear consequences</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q8" value="q8d" id="q8d">
        <label for="q8d">That consequences do not actually matter, only intentions do</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Mill says humanity has had its whole history to learn the tendencies of actions. Our inherited moral rules record that experience, so we need not calculate from scratch every time.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Mill does not deny that consequences matter or that they are hard to predict. Ask what he thinks humanity's accumulated experience gives us.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q9a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">9</span> Mill's account of the ultimate sanction of morality grounds moral motivation in:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9a" id="q9a">
        <label for="q9a">The natural sociability of human beings — our deep psychological tendency to identify with others</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9b" id="q9b">
        <label for="q9b">A separate moral faculty given to us in advance, prior to social experience</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9c" id="q9c">
        <label for="q9c">Fear of legal punishment</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q9" value="q9d" id="q9d">
        <label for="q9d">Religious belief in divine reward and punishment</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Mill grounds the ultimate sanction in human social feeling, our natural desire to be in unity with others, which education and social progress can strengthen.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Mill argues the deepest motive to be moral is not fear of punishment, religion, or an innate moral faculty. Ask what feature of human nature he appeals to in Chapter 3.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz__question" data-correct="q10a">
    <div class="quiz__question-text"><span class="quiz__number">10</span> In Mill's terminology, internal sanctions refer to:</div>
    <ul class="quiz__choices">
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10a" id="q10a">
        <label for="q10a">The pressures we exert on ourselves, such as conscience and the feeling of guilt</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10b" id="q10b">
        <label for="q10b">Divine sanction in the form of reward and punishment after death</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10c" id="q10c">
        <label for="q10c">Legal penalties imposed by the state</label>
      </li>
      <li class="quiz__choice">
        <input type="radio" name="q10" value="q10d" id="q10d">
        <label for="q10d">Social pressure from one's community</label>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--correct" style="display:none;">Correct. Internal sanctions are the feelings within us: conscience and the pain of violating duty. External sanctions come from others: the law, public opinion, or God.</div>
    <div class="quiz__feedback quiz__feedback--incorrect" style="display:none;">Mill divides sanctions into external and internal. Ask which kind arises within the person's own mind.</div>
  </div>
</div>

